\section{Research attempt: Scaffold generation needs more than valid individual files}
Consider a generator producing two linked files, a syllabus $s$ and worksheet $w$, with a reference from $s$ to $w$. If it writes $s$ successfully and then fails before writing $w$, each completed file may be syntactically valid, yet the generated project is incomplete. Prevalidating the two file contents does not eliminate this failure between writes.

In an abstract transactional filesystem, a stronger algorithm is: reserve a fresh destination namespace exclusively; create all files in a private staging area; validate the finite manifest and its internal references; atomically publish the completed namespace. Assuming atomic publication and that failures before publication leave staging invisible, every visible new project is complete. The proof has two cases. Before the atomic event the new project is not visible; after it, all manifest entries have already been written and checked. There is no model state in which only a proper prefix of its files is publicly visible.

The freshness check also needs an atomic reservation under concurrency. A separate check-then-write is insufficient: two generators can both observe an absent name, then both write it, with one overwriting the other's content. This interleaving obeys both local prechecks. Exclusive reservation permits at most one successful creator of that name, so the losing operation can report a collision before modifying the reserved project. These are assumptions of a selected abstract service, not evidence that any actual CLI implements the protocol.

Even the proven transaction property does not choose a useful syllabus schema or worksheet pedagogy. It only prevents a specific partial-creation and overwrite route from being mistaken for a successfully generated component collection.
\section{Verification and adversarial audit}
The construction and counterexample are checked in the written finite model. No actual PreTeXt build, browser, EPUB reader, or publishing service was tested. All runtime and identity assumptions used by the proof are stated explicitly.
\paragraph{Audit corrections.} Atomic publication and exclusive reservation are explicit premises. Content validation, project completeness, and concurrency correctness are separate claims.
\paragraph{External computation.} None; symbolic arguments and boundary checks only.
\section{Scope and remaining gaps}
This is a restricted route investigation. The full feature requires implementation, supported-version decisions, conformance tests, and user-facing evaluation that were not performed here. The original agenda remains unresolved, and no novelty or completed software feature is claimed.
\paragraph{Integration review.} Codex (GPT-6; exact serving revision not exposed) reviewed the written arguments and expanded layout and source-reference wrappers. Repository metadata and the production web renderer were checked using Python 3.13 and JavaScript with MathJax 3.2.2. This model-assisted review is not independent human proof verification; no PDF compilation or formal proof checking is asserted.

